---
import { Code } from 'astro:components';
import { farmTheme } from '../lib/farm-shiki-theme.mjs';

const rows = [
  {
    label: 'Service model',
    vgi: 'Application-defined methods; native types or Arrow schemas in code',
    grpc: 'Application-defined methods; .proto definitions and generated stubs by default',
    flight: 'Standard data-service API: discovery, get, put, exchange, and custom actions',
  },
  {
    label: 'Payload format',
    vgi: 'Apache Arrow IPC record batches',
    grpc: 'Protocol Buffers by default; custom serializers are possible',
    flight: 'Arrow IPC data with Protobuf-defined Flight messages',
  },
  {
    label: 'Transport',
    vgi: 'Pipes, subprocesses, Unix/TCP, HTTP, Iroh; shared-memory side channel',
    grpc: 'HTTP/2-based gRPC',
    flight: 'gRPC by default; the specification also allows alternative transports',
  },
  {
    label: 'Browser access',
    vgi: 'TypeScript client calls the HTTP endpoint directly with fetch',
    grpc: 'gRPC-Web client plus a compatible endpoint or translating proxy',
    flight: 'The standard gRPC endpoint needs a browser-compatible client and gateway or adapter',
  },
  {
    label: 'WebAssembly',
    vgi: 'Rust HTTP client supports a host-provided executor, including Emscripten integration',
    grpc: 'Depends on the client and runtime; browser WASM still needs browser-compatible networking',
    flight: 'Depends on the client and runtime; compiling to WASM alone does not adapt gRPC for browsers',
  },
  {
    label: 'Streaming model',
    vgi: 'Producer streams and lockstep request/response exchange',
    grpc: 'Unary, client, server, and bidirectional streaming; gRPC-Web has a smaller supported subset',
    flight: 'DoGet, DoPut, and DoExchange; browser support depends on the adapter',
  },
];
const browserExample = `import { httpConnect } from "@query-farm/vgi-rpc";

const client = httpConnect("https://api.example.com", {
  protocol: "Calculator",
});
try {
  const result = await client.call("add", { a: 2, b: 3 });
  console.log(result); // { result: 5 }
} finally {
  client.close();
}`;
---

<section id="comparison" class="py-20 md:py-24 bg-soil-100 dark:bg-rock-950/50">
  <div class="max-w-7xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">
    <h2 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl text-soil-900 dark:text-cream">Where vgi-rpc fits.</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-lg text-soil-700 dark:text-cream-2">
      Arrow-native application RPC, from local workers to browser clients.
      Here is how the architecture differs from gRPC and Arrow Flight.
    </p>

    <div class="comparison-context">
      <div>
        <h3>Alongside gRPC</h3>
        <p>gRPC provides cross-language service calls and generated, typed clients. vgi-rpc makes Arrow batches
          the native payload and lets the protocol run over a subprocess pipe as well as an HTTP connection.</p>
        <a class="qf-link" href="https://grpc.io/docs/what-is-grpc/introduction/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">gRPC architecture &nearr;</a>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3>Alongside Arrow Flight</h3>
        <p>Flight already transfers Arrow batches efficiently over gRPC. vgi-rpc exposes your own application methods
          over multiple transports, including an HTTP API that a browser client can call directly.</p>
        <a class="qf-link" href="https://arrow.apache.org/docs/format/Flight.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Arrow Flight architecture &nearr;</a>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="comparison-scroll overflow-x-auto" role="region" aria-label="Comparison of vgi-rpc, gRPC, and Arrow Flight" tabindex="0">
      <table class="rpc-comparison">
        <caption class="sr-only">Service definitions, payloads, transports, browser and WebAssembly access, and streaming models.</caption>
        <thead><tr>
          <th scope="col">Architecture</th>
          <th scope="col" class="comparison-vgi"><span class="font-mono">vgi-rpc</span></th>
          <th scope="col">gRPC</th>
          <th scope="col">Arrow Flight</th>
        </tr></thead>
        <tbody>{rows.map(row => <tr>
          <th scope="row">{row.label}</th>
          <td class="comparison-vgi">{row.vgi}</td>
          <td>{row.grpc}</td>
          <td>{row.flight}</td>
        </tr>)}</tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <div class="browser-comparison">
      <div class="browser-comparison-copy">
        <h3>An HTTP endpoint your browser can call.</h3>
        <p>The vgi-rpc TypeScript client sends Arrow IPC using browser HTTP APIs.
          Point it at your service and configure CORS for cross-origin requests.
          There is no gRPC-Web translation layer to deploy.</p>
        <p>gRPC and Flight can be reached from browsers through suitable adapters.
          The distinction is the direct HTTP path: compiling a native gRPC client to WASM
          does not give it access to networking features the browser does not expose.</p>
        <a class="qf-link" href="https://github.com/grpc/grpc-web#readme" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">How gRPC-Web connects browser clients &nearr;</a>
      </div>
      <div class="browser-example">
        <div class="example-file">
          <div class="example-file-heading"><span class="example-filename">browser.ts</span><span class="example-caption">vgi-rpc over HTTPS</span></div>
          <Code code={browserExample} lang="typescript" theme={farmTheme} />
        </div>
        <p class="example-setup">Calls the Calculator service from the example above once it is hosted over HTTPS.
          Bundle with Vite, esbuild, or another browser bundler.</p>
        <a class="qf-link" href="https://github.com/Query-farm/vgi-rpc-typescript#browser-usage" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">TypeScript browser setup &nearr;</a>
      </div>
    </div>

    <p class="comparison-footnote">
      For WASM hosts, the Rust client accepts a custom HTTP executor; the application supplies its runtime’s networking integration.
      WASM support depends on the selected SDK and host runtime.{' '}
      <a class="qf-link" href="https://github.com/Query-farm/vgi-rpc-rust/tree/main/vgi-rpc-client#custom-http-executor" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Rust HTTP executor integration &nearr;</a>
    </p>
    <p class="comparison-footnote">
      Transport and feature availability varies by implementation. See the <a class="qf-link" href="#capabilities">SDK capability matrix</a>{' '}
      and the <a class="qf-link" href="https://vgi-rpc-python.query.farm/comparison/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">broader framework comparison &nearr;</a>.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>
